{"id":278679915055177,"type":"album","name":"Monster Movie","artist":"Can","year":1969,"weight":27,"info":{"cover":"https://dn710306.ca.archive.org/0/items/mbid-b5354db6-ed39-4d5f-883c-6c73e4034dda/mbid-b5354db6-ed39-4d5f-883c-6c73e4034dda-11639614531.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":4,"country":"XW","scaruffi":7.0,"yt":"ZeiycXs5NMs","links":{"spotify":"https://open.spotify.com/album/3aMBDb72H0wrXO5iARzsWc","youtube":"https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=OLAK5uy_mfenwwcWDbbdsn_bekWejbT4grm6Cmo-4","bandcamp":"https://canofficial.bandcamp.com/album/monster-movie","rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/the-can/monster-movie"},"desc":"Monster Movie is the debut album by Can. Some copies of the LP bore the subtitle \"Made in a castle with better equipment\". Upon its release in 1969, the album became very influential in the development of Krautrock. It is notable for being the only Can album (until the 1989 reunion on Rite Time) on which Malcolm Mooney performed all of the vocals. Mooney recorded a few songs for the following Soundtracks album and was replaced by Damo Suzuki. The cover of the album depicts a faceless Galactus, and is credited to \"The Can\", a name originally suggested by Malcolm ."},"artist_ref":{"id":126657727149758,"name":"The Can","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Can_1972_%28Heinrich_Klaffs_Collection_102%29.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":147423806094006,"type":"genre","name":"Psychedelic Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":690,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":4451667526661,"type":"genre","name":"Krautrock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":418,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":173341470623130,"type":"genre","name":"avant-garde","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":298,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":262736572970899,"type":"genre","name":"Space Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":282,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":166905196183001,"type":"genre","name":"psychedelic","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":138,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":55893123468232,"type":"genre","name":"Experimental Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":373,"yt":null,"src":"rym"}],"lists":[{"id":23428794615065,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Krautrock","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":10,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":7.0},{"id":32093215757431,"name":"Pitchfork: The 200 Best Albums of the 1960s","src":"musthear","grp":"musicbrainz","list_year":null,"rank":48,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":7.0},{"id":185548171992260,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Psychedelic Rock","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":66,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":7.0},{"id":22478217896300,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Experimental Rock","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":91,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":7.0},{"id":170088453171885,"name":"Scaruffi 1960s","src":"charts","grp":"Scaruffi","list_year":null,"rank":98,"year":1969,"note":null,"total":207,"scaruffi":7.0}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Can's first official album, described as a \"table de boot\" made more accessible to help the band secure a record deal. It featured three songs on one side and the 20-minute track \"Yoo Doo Right\" on the other, establishing a tribal mantra driven by Jaki Liebezeit's drums. This album was a clear statement of intent, blending rock, funk, and free jazz influences with a crucial emphasis on percussions over melodies, a dynamic that would define their future releases and the distinct sound of Kraut Rock. \"una table de boot al cual hicieron un más accesible para que no tuviesen problemas a la banda de encontrar discográfica.\"","year":1969,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Central Sonora","episode":"CAN ｜ La Banda que (posiblemente) Inspiró a tu banda preferida","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4qHiw2wHWAI"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":27867991505